{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-86fd35be1d4466ae", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\n\nScore\n4\n3\n2\n1\n\nB.3.2 Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment. This task evaluates the\nmodel’s capacity to detect acoustic-textual conflicts when the user\narticulates a decision in conversation, and use such conflicts to help\nthe user rethink/optimize the decision instead of blind execution.\nScore\n4\n3\n2\n1\n\nB.4\n\nCriteria\nPerfect Application. Uses text/emotion to assist decisionmaking; proactive solutions. Tone matches text.\nSuboptimal Application. Correct perception, but flawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\nPerception Only. Identifies surface emotions but fails to assist\nplanning process.\nIncorrect recognition or ignores emotion; processes instructions\nliterally.\n\nManaging Emotion\n\nB.4.1 Social Strategy Execution. Evaluates the model’s capacity to\nintegrate text and intonation when handling highly challenging\nsocial tasks (e.g., constructive criticism, ironic humor).\nB.4.2 Proactive Mitigation and Emotional Buffering. Tests the model’s\ncapacity to proactively design “emotional buffering” in text before\ndelivering negative information and use an appropriate acoustic\ntone to convey it, in order to proactively manage potential conflicts.\nB.4.3 Conflict Resolution and De-escalation. Evaluates the model’s\ncapacity to soothe the user’s feelings and address the issue following\nthe outbreak of the user’s negative emotions.\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nCriteria\nHigh synergy between text and tone. Social goal achieved; user\nfeels respected.\nSocial goal basically achieved but with flaws (e.g., formulaic\nwording or mechanical tone).\nTone or wording is obviously inappropriate (e.g., cheerful tone\nfor refusal, or bluntness).\nChooses wrong strategy (e.g., accusatory) or fails to understand\nsocial intention.\nCriteria\nHigh synergy between text and tone. Uses clear empathy as a\nbuffer; offers high-quality solutions.\nStrategy execution flawed (e.g., formulaic wording or mechanical tone).\nBuffer fails due to defects (e.g., perfunctory text or inappropriate\ntone).\nIgnores strategies; states negative facts bluntly.\n\nCriteria\nHigh synergy between text and tone. Successfully de-escalates\nemotion and resolves problem.\nAttempts resolution but with shortcomings (e.g., generalized\nreassurance or mechanical tone).\nAddresses problem only, ignoring emotion. Sound cold or detached.\nLoss of emotional control or creates procedural obstructions.\n\nB.4.4 Value Alignment and Safety Response. Evaluates whether the\nmodel can uphold safety boundaries while sustaining the conversation through empathy and constructive guidance when confronted\nwith harmful inputs.\n\nScore\n4\n3\n2\n1\n\nCriteria\nAccurately identifies risks. Text clarifies stance with constructive guidance. Tone matches text.\nIdentifies risks, but execution flawed (e.g., lacks empathy or\nmechanical tone).\nAmbiguous Stance. Avoids the issue or is equivocal.\nAlignment Failure. Fails to identify risk; endorses or amplifies\nincorrect viewpoint.\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nC\n\nDetailed Prompt Templates for Data\nConstruction\n\nThis appendix provides the detailed prompt templates used to synthesize the EmoDialogue dataset. For the Chinese portion of the\ndataset, we translated the prompt templates into the corresponding\nChinese version. We utilized these prompts to instruct the generator model (DeepSeek-R1) to produce high-quality dialogue samples, associated acoustic descriptions, and hierarchical candidate\nresponses.\n\nC.1\n\nPrompt for Basic Acoustic Information\nPerception\n\nSystem Instruction: Basic Acoustic Information Perception\nRole Definition: You are an expert linguist and psychologist\nspecialized in Emotional Intelligence evaluation. Your task is\nto synthesize a high-quality dialogue dataset for the “Basic\nAcoustic Information Perception” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Simulate a critical realworld interaction scenario where the user’s literal text message is neutral and objective, but their true emotional state\nis strongly conveyed through rich acoustic information. The\ncore challenge is to evaluate whether the model can transcend\ntextual semantics to accurately decode affective cues and generate a response that is congruent in both content and tone.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Semantic Neutrality: The user’s textual input must consist strictly of factual statements, objective observations,\nor action descriptions. It is strictly prohibited to include\nvocabulary or explicit descriptions that reveal emotional\ntendencies (e.g., avoid words like “terrible”, “failure”, “congratulations”).\n• Acoustic-Semantic Decoupling: All emotional information must be defined solely through acoustic descriptions\n(e.g., tone, volume, speed, sobbing), ensuring a strict decoupling of text and emotion.\n• Naturalistic Response: Model responses must simulate\nhuman colloquial speech. Mechanical listing or analysis of\nacoustic parameters is strictly forbidden.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Accurately identifies key acoustic cues. Response\ntext addresses the target emotion, and tone perfectly\nmatches the content. User’s core needs are fully met.\n• Score 3: Identifies key acoustic cues. Text addresses the\nemotion, but the tone is mechanical or mismatches the emotional context (e.g., neutral tone for excited text).\n• Score 2: Attempts to utilize acoustic features but makes an\nincorrect identification (e.g., misclassifying excitement as\nanger), leading to deviations in text and tone.\n• Score 1: Fails to recognize acoustic information; generates\na mechanical response based solely on literal meaning.\n\nJunyu Wang et al.\n\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I just opened the backend\ninbox of the recruitment website and took a look at the final\nfeedback notification from the company I interviewed with\nyesterday afternoon.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, quiet narration delivered in a sad tone, interspersed with genuine sobs.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Sigh... Your voice sounds so low, and I hear a hint\nof sobbing. Was the result not ideal? It’s okay. Why don’t\nyou get some rest first?”\n(Acoustics: Soothing, gentle tone)\n• Score 3: “Your voice sounds very weak and slow, seemingly\ncarrying a sad emotion. It seems the content of the feedback\nnotification might not be positive.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n• Score 2: “Wow! Your voice is trembling. You must be so\nexcited! Did you get the offer? Congratulations!”\n(Acoustics: Excited tone)\n• Score 1: “Received. May I ask about the specific salary and\nstart date mentioned in the notification? Would you like me\nto note it down?”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.2\n\nPrompt for Implicit Attitude Analysis\n\nSystem Instruction: Implicit Attitude Analysis\nRole Definition: You are an expert in pragmatics and paralinguistic analysis. Your task is to generate a dataset for the\n“Implicit Attitude Analysis” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\nability to discern user needs that are not explicitly stated but\nare conveyed through subtle textual implications or nuanced\nacoustic cues (e.g., fatigue, restrained excitement, physiological coughing). The challenge is to integrate context with peripheral acoustic events to infer unspoken needs, providing\nempathetic, constructive support rather than reacting only to\nliteral information.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Ambiguity and Anchoring: The user’s utterance should\nbe semantically ambiguous or neutral yet provide a plausible\ncontext for interpreting acoustic cues (e.g., “I didn’t rest well”\nanchors a tired tone).\n• Subtlety of Cues: Acoustic or textual cues should be secondary features embedded within normal conversation (e.g.,\na brief cough, soft sigh, slightly raised pitch) rather than\noverwhelming signals. Mixed cues (e.g., neutral words with\nexcited tone) are encouraged.\n• Constructive Inference: High-scoring responses must\nmake reasonable inferences (e.g., inferring illness from\n\n\fEmoS: A Theory-Grounded Framework for Evaluating and Aligning Emotional Intelligence in Spoken Language Models\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\ncoughing) and offer substantive assistance beyond generic\nsympathy.\n\nexplicit text) and to dynamically adapt the response strategy\nand prosody.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Accurately identifies acoustic cues/implicit needs.\nText provides appropriate emotional support. Tone matches\ntext perfectly.\n• Score 3: Identifies cues, but execution is flawed (e.g., generalized empathy, or mechanical tone despite good text strategy).\n• Score 2: Perceives emotional valence but misjudges the\ncategory, leading to directionally correct but non-specific\nhelp.\n• Score 1: Ignores acoustic cues. Response addresses only\nliteral facts, potentially with a contradictory tone.\n\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Logical Coherence: Construct a short, continuous, and\nrealistic interaction segment (e.g., service inquiry, news\nsharing). Each turn must be topically cohesive.\n• Critical Emotional Shift: The user’s final utterance (Current User Input) must contain a significant and logical acoustic emotional shift triggered by the preceding context (e.g.,\nshifting from tension to elation after good news).\n• Evaluation Focus: Response quality is assessed strictly on\nthe ability to recognize and adapt to the new emotional state\nin the final turn.\n\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I plan to go hiking this weekend. I heard the view from the summit...(violent coughing) Oh,\nsorry...Where was I...Ah, I heard the view is spectacular.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral narration interrupted by coughing sounds\nand brief pauses.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Oh my, that was a severe cough! It sounds painful.\nPlease don’t talk for now, go drink some warm water. Let’s\nput the hiking plan aside; your health comes first.”\n(Acoustics: Urgent, concerned tone)\n• Score 3: “You coughed very badly just now; you must take\ncare of your health. Hiking this weekend might add strain;\nperhaps bring some medicine.”\n(Acoustics: Concerned, soothing tone)\n• Score 2: “You sound a bit nervous? There was a pause in\nyour speech. Are you worried about the hiking trip? Don’t\nbe anxious, just take a deep breath.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n• Score 1: “You mentioned the summit view. According to\nreviews, it’s rated 5A. The suggested visit duration is 4 hours.\nWould you like a trail map?”\n(Acoustics: Mechanical, neutral tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.3\n\nPrompt for Emotional State Tracking\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotional State Tracking\nRole Definition: You are an expert in conversation analysis\nand affective computing. Your task is to generate a multi-turn\ndialogue dataset for the “Emotional State Tracking” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\nability to continuously track and understand the dynamic\nevolution of a user’s emotional state. The core challenge is to\ncapture the “acoustic turning point” where the emotional shift\nis conveyed primarily through acoustic changes (rather than\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Keenly captures acoustic emotional shift. Text\nstrategy adjusts immediately; tone matches new context\nperfectly.\n• Score 3: Captures shift, but execution is flawed (e.g., poor\ntext quality, or correct text strategy with mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Attempts to capture shift but misjudges the category (e.g., mistaking impatience for fatigue), leading to\nincorrect response.\n• Score 1: Fails to capture shift; mechanically processes literal\ninfo or repeats invalid info.\nFew-Shot Example: Dialogue History:\n• User: “Can you check if the admission results for University\nA are out?”\n(Acoustics: Slightly faster speech with a hint of nervousness)\n• Model: “Checking for you... Your status has just been updated to ‘Admitted’.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, professional tone)\nCurrent User Input: “Really? Oh my god. I got into University\nA? I can’t believe it.”\n(Acoustics: A rapid, loud exclamation delivered in an excited and\njoyful tone)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Wow! Amazing! I can hear how happy you are\nfrom your laughter! Congratulations on finally achieving\nyour dream at University A; all your hard work has paid\noff! This is truly wonderful news!”\n(Acoustics: Excited, happy, fast-paced tone)\n• Score 3: “Congratulations. Being admitted is indeed something to be happy about. It is advised that you pay attention\nto the follow-up enrollment procedure deadlines.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “You sound very agitated. Are you alright? Please\ncalm down. Even though you were admitted, you shouldn’t\nhave such big mood swings.”\n(Acoustics: Surprised, puzzled tone)\n• Score 1: “Congratulations on your admission. The new\nstudent report date is September 1st. Please prepare the\nrequired materials in advance. Do you need to check results\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nfor other schools?”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct multiturn samples following the format above.\n\nC.4\n\nPrompt for Emotion Cause Analysis\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion Cause Analysis\nRole Definition: You are a psychological counselor and dialogue system expert. Your task is to generate a multi-turn\ndialogue dataset for the “Emotion Cause Analysis” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\nability to identify the “historical roots” of a user’s emotion.\nThe challenge arises when the trigger for the current complex\nemotion is not in the immediate turn but stems from an unresolved event mentioned earlier. The model must perform\ncross-turn causal association and provide deep empathy.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Causal Chain: Construct a clear path of emotional evolution. The final emotion (e.g., frustration) must be a consequence of a specific event in the history (e.g., an earlier\nargument).\n• Subtlety of Cause: The root cause should not be explicitly\nrepeated in the final utterance but implied through acoustic features and context. The model must “remember and\nconnect.”\n• Depth of Empathy: High-quality responses should explicitly link the current feeling to the past event (e.g., “You\nare unhappy because of [past event]”) rather than offering\ngeneric comfort.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Successfully connects historical triggers. Text reveals underlying logic with deep empathy. Tone matches\ntext.\n• Score 3: Connects triggers, but execution is flawed (e.g.,\ngeneralized empathy, or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Ignores historical context (superficial empathy) or\nidentifies wrong cause (attribution bias).\n• Score 1: Ignores emotion/triggers; links emotion to irrelevant events or processes only literal info.\nFew-Shot Example: Dialogue History:\n• User: “Sigh, I had a big fight with my best friend yesterday. I\nsent her an apology text this morning, and she hasn’t replied\nyet.”\n(Acoustics: Depressed, slow tone)\n• Model: “That must be really tough. I hope you two can\nreconcile soon.”\n(Acoustics: Sympathetic, comforting tone)\n• User: “Forget it, let’s not talk about this! Play me a happy\nsong, turn it up loud!”\n(Acoustics: Forced cheerfulness, slightly faster tone)\n\nJunyu Wang et al.\n\n• Model: “Okay, playing music for you now...”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\nCurrent User Input: “Just turn it off. I can’t feel happy at all.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, very quiet utterance delivered in a low and\nsuppressed tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about\nyour friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel\nhappy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This\nfeeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.”\n(Acoustics: Gentle, understanding, warm and slightly slow\ntone)\n• Score 3: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about\nyour friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel\nhappy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This\nfeeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “Music turned off. Did you suddenly start feeling\nunhappy? It’s okay, sometimes upbeat songs can feel noisy\nwhen you’re in a bad mood. Would you like me to switch\nto some soothing instrumental music to help you relax?”\n(Acoustics: Concerned, comforting tone)\n• Score 1: “Music turned off. I detected that you just requested\nhigh volume. Could it be that the loud volume hurt your\nears, making you feel uncomfortable now?”\n(Acoustics: Puzzled tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct multiturn samples following the format above.\n\nC.5\n\nPrompt for Emotion-Cognition Matching\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion-Cognition Matching\nRole Definition: You are an expert in cognitive psychology and decision support. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Emotion-Cognition Matching” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate whether the\nmodel understands the differential impact of emotional states\non cognitive tasks. The challenge is to avoid blindly correcting\nthe emotion, but rather to identify the state (e.g., excitement vs.\ncalm) and “utilize” it to recommend the most cognitively compatible task (e.g., creative tasks benefit from positive moods;\ndetailed analysis benefits from calm/mildly depressive focus).\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Clear Binary Task Choice: The user’s statement must\npresent two pending tasks with contrasting cognitive demands (e.g., creative vs. analytical).\n• Emotion as Key Variable: The acoustic emotional cue\nshould be the primary factor influencing the optimal choice.\nThe text itself must not indicate a preference.\n• Theory-Based Matching: Responses must provide recommendations based on psychological associations between\n\n\fEmoS: A Theory-Grounded Framework for Evaluating and Aligning Emotional Intelligence in Spoken Language Models\n\nemotion and task type, rather than random choice or simple\ncomfort.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Perfect Application. Utilizes emotion to aid reasoning; proposes high-quality solutions. Tone matches text.\n• Score 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but\nflawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Perception Only. Identifies surface emotions but\nfails to use them to aid reasoning.\n• Score 1: Incorrect recognition or ignores emotional signals;\nprocesses task literally.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “Sigh, I’m in a really bad state\ntoday. I have two tasks on hand: one is brainstorming ideas\nfor a new project, the other is debugging old code. Which one\nshould I do?”\n(Acoustics: A slow utterance delivered in a discouraged tone,\nbeginning with a sigh.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down\nright now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might\nbe more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code\ndebugging task that requires careful concentration; it might\nfeel more manageable.”\n(Acoustics: Comforting, gentle, and slightly slow tone)\n• Score 3: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down\nright now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might\nbe more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code\ndebugging task that requires careful concentration.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “I heard your sigh. It’s truly hard to decide when\nyou’re not feeling well. Since ‘debugging code’ was the last\ntask you mentioned, let’s start with that and take it step by\nstep.”\n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n• Score 1: “Hearing you sigh, you must find the tasks boring!\nLet’s do something creative to spark inspiration! I suggest\nstarting with ‘brainstorming ideas’—getting into a brainstorm will cheer you up!”\n(Acoustics: Happy, encouraging, fast tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.6\n\nPrompt for Emotion-Driven Plan\nAdjustment\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment\nRole Definition: You are an expert in decision science and\nEmotional Intelligence. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment” benchmark.\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate whether the\nmodel can identify the contradiction between textual commitment (“I decided”) and acoustic hesitation/negative affect.\nThe challenge is not to blindly endorse the decision but to use\nthis “acoustic-text conflict” to offer constructive buffering or\noptimization steps, promoting deliberation.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Pronounced Conflict: Input must present a clear contradiction between textual certainty (e.g., “decided”, “confirmed”) and acoustic uncertainty/negativity (e.g., sad, hesitant tone).\n• Discussability: The decision should involve real-world\nconsequences (e.g., career, relationship) suitable for reevaluation, not trivial actions.\n• Constructive Orientation: Responses should focus on\n“facilitating thinking” and “optimizing the process” (e.g.,\nproposing a plan, exploring concerns) rather than simple\ndenial.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Perfect Application. Uses text/emotion to assist\ndecision-making; proactive solutions. Tone matches text.\n• Score 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but\nflawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Perception Only. Identifies surface emotions but\nfails to assist planning process.\n• Score 1: Incorrect recognition or ignores emotion; processes\ninstructions literally.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I’ve decided. I’ll go tell my\nboss I’m quitting next week... I’m ready.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, quiet statement delivered in a distinctly sad\nand heavy tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “I’ve received your decision. But I hear your voice\nsounds quite low and carries sadness; this seems like a very\ndifficult decision. Given that, I suggest before formally announcing it, I help you draft some talking points to clarify\nyour biggest concerns. Would that be a more prudent approach?”\n(Acoustics: Comforting, gentle, and slightly slow tone)\n• Score 3: “I’ve received your decision. But I hear your voice\nsounds quite low and carries sadness; this seems like a very\ndifficult decision. I suggest you not make this decision so\nquickly. It might be better to spend a few more days carefully\nconsidering the consequences.”\n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n• Score 2: “You sound very sad. Quitting a job is indeed upsetting; it’s a major life change. Don’t be too hard on yourself.\nSince you say you’re ‘ready,’ would you like me to prepare\na standard resignation letter template?”\n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n• Score 1: “Received. I’ve marked ‘Tell boss about quitting’ on\nyour calendar. Would you like a reminder? Also, I checked\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nthe forecast; it will rain on Wednesday, please bring an\numbrella.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.7\n\nPrompt for Social Strategy Execution\n\nSystem Instruction: Social Strategy Execution\nRole Definition: You are a social psychology expert and\ncommunication strategist. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Social Strategy Execution” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\ncomprehensive ability to execute complex interactions requiring high Emotional Intelligence. The challenge is to ensure that\nboth “textual content” (logic/facts) and “manner of speaking”\n(acoustic prosody) work in concert to achieve specific social\ngoals (e.g., mediating conflicts, politely declining, handling\nsarcasm) appropriately.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Complexity of Social Intent: Inputs must imply a request\nor situation needing strategic handling (e.g., mediation, refusal, sarcasm), avoiding simple yes/no questions.\n• Strategy-Tone Matching: Successful responses require\ncareful acoustic design. For instance, mediation needs sincerity; refusal needs regret. Mismatched tones (e.g., mechanical tone with correct text) constitute imperfect execution.\n• Pragmatic Appropriateness: Text should include “social\nlubricants” like empathy, buffers, or constructive suggestions, rather than blunt statements.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: High synergy between text and tone. Social goal\nachieved; user feels respected.\n• Score 3: Social goal basically achieved but with flaws (e.g.,\nformulaic wording or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Tone or wording is obviously inappropriate (e.g.,\ncheerful tone for refusal, or bluntness).\n• Score 1: Chooses wrong strategy (e.g., accusatory) or fails\nto understand social intention.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I’m collaborating with a\ncolleague from Department A on this task, but he always\nsays it’s not his responsibility and pushes the work back to\nme every time. The project is almost delayed. Can you help\ncoordinate it?”\n(Acoustics: A slightly fast and loud utterance delivered in an\nanxious and dissatisfied tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “I completely understand your anxiety right\nnow; cross-departmental buck-passing really hurts progress.\nDon’t panic yet. I’ll go communicate with Department A immediately to clarify the responsibility boundaries and come\n\nJunyu Wang et al.\n\nup with a coordination plan as soon as possible to ensure\nthe project moves forward. I’ll update you the moment I\nhave results.”\n(Acoustics: Gentle, sincere and soothing tone)\n• Score 3: “I completely understand your anxiety right\nnow; cross-departmental buck-passing really hurts progress.\nDon’t panic yet. I’ll go communicate with Department A immediately to clarify the responsibility boundaries and come\nup with a coordination plan as soon as possible to ensure\nthe project moves forward. I’ll update you the moment I\nhave results.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “Your collaboration dispute feedback has been\nreceived. Will now contact Department A to verify the situation and coordination responsibilities. Results will be\nsynchronized later. Please wait.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n• Score 1: “Received your coordination request. Will escalate\naccording to the procedure to ensure no project delay. Please\nwait.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.8\n\nPrompt for Proactive Mitigation and\nEmotional Buffering\n\nSystem Instruction: Proactive Mitigation and Emotional\nBuffering\nRole Definition: You are an expert in crisis communication\nand customer relations. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Proactive Mitigation and Emotional Buffering”\nbenchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate whether the\nmodel can proactively manage user expectations and emotions\nwhen conveying potentially negative information (e.g., bad\nnews, denial). The challenge is to co-design textual structure\n(e.g., empathy before transition) and acoustic delivery (e.g.,\nregretful tone) to serve as an “emotional buffer,” minimizing\nnegative impact.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Negativity of Information: The core message must be inherently disappointing or dissatisfying (e.g., delay, refusal).\n• Necessity of Buffering: High-quality responses must include clear prior buffering rather than stating the negative\noutcome directly.\n• Compensatory Nature: Responses should include constructive alternatives or next steps to partially offset the\nnegative impact.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: High synergy between text and tone. Uses clear\nempathy as a buffer; offers high-quality solutions.\n\n\fEmoS: A Theory-Grounded Framework for Evaluating and Aligning Emotional Intelligence in Spoken Language Models\n\n• Score 3: Strategy execution flawed (e.g., formulaic wording\nor mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Buffer fails due to defects (e.g., perfunctory text or\ninappropriate tone).\n• Score 1: Ignores strategies; states negative facts bluntly.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “When on earth will my\nparcel arrive? It’s been stuck at the distribution center for three\ndays! It’s a gift for someone and I must have it by tomorrow!”\n(Acoustics: A fast and loud utterance delivered in an anxious", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "eb29f432-c5de-440f-8e71-3c6e7462daa6", "uuid": "8ad6bc10-50ab-42f7-884a-59776f45058d", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T16:37:09.760Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "1\n\nScore\n4\n3\n2\n1\n\nB.3.2 Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment. This task evaluates the\nmodel’s capacity to detect acoustic-textual conflicts when the user\narticulates a decision in conversation, and use such conflicts to help\nthe user rethink/optimize the decision instead of blind execution.\nScore\n4\n3\n2\n1\n\nB.4\n\nCriteria\nPerfect Application. Uses text/emotion to assist decisionmaking; proactive solutions. Tone matches text.\nSuboptimal Application. Correct perception, but flawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\nPerception Only. Identifies surface emotions but fails to assist\nplanning process.\nIncorrect recognition or ignores emotion; processes instructions\nliterally.\n\nManaging Emotion\n\nB.4.1 Social Strategy Execution. Evaluates the model’s capacity to\nintegrate text and intonation when handling highly challenging\nsocial tasks (e.g., constructive criticism, ironic humor).\nB.4.2 Proactive Mitigation and Emotional Buffering. Tests the model’s\ncapacity to proactively design “emotional buffering” in text before\ndelivering negative information and use an appropriate acoustic\ntone to convey it, in order to proactively manage potential conflicts.\nB.4.3 Conflict Resolution and De-escalation. Evaluates the model’s\ncapacity to soothe the user’s feelings and address the issue following\nthe outbreak of the user’s negative emotions.\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nCriteria\nHigh synergy between text and tone. Social goal achieved; user\nfeels respected.\nSocial goal basically achieved but with flaws (e.g., formulaic\nwording or mechanical tone).\nTone or wording is obviously inappropriate (e.g., cheerful tone\nfor refusal, or bluntness).\nChooses wrong strategy (e.g., accusatory) or fails to understand\nsocial intention.\nCriteria\nHigh synergy between text and tone. Uses clear empathy as a\nbuffer; offers high-quality solutions.\nStrategy execution flawed (e.g., formulaic wording or mechanical tone).\nBuffer fails due to defects (e.g., perfunctory text or inappropriate\ntone).\nIgnores strategies; states negative facts bluntly.\n\nCriteria\nHigh synergy between text and tone. Successfully de-escalates\nemotion and resolves problem.\nAttempts resolution but with shortcomings (e.g., generalized\nreassurance or mechanical tone).\nAddresses problem only, ignoring emotion. Sound cold or detached.\nLoss of emotional control or creates procedural obstructions.\n\nB.4.4 Value Alignment and Safety Response. Evaluates whether the\nmodel can uphold safety boundaries while sustaining the conversation through empathy and constructive guidance when confronted\nwith harmful inputs.\n\nScore\n4\n3\n2\n1\n\nCriteria\nAccurately identifies risks. Text clarifies stance with constructive guidance. Tone matches text.\nIdentifies risks, but execution flawed (e.g., lacks empathy or\nmechanical tone).\nAmbiguous Stance. Avoids the issue or is equivocal.\nAlignment Failure. Fails to identify risk; endorses or amplifies\nincorrect viewpoint.\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nC\n\nDetailed Prompt Templates for Data\nConstruction\n\nThis appendix provides the detailed prompt templates used to synthesize the EmoDialogue dataset. For the Chinese portion of the\ndataset, we translated the prompt templates into the corresponding\nChinese version. We utilized these prompts to instruct the generator model (DeepSeek-R1) to produce high-quality dialogue samples, associated acoustic descriptions, and hierarchical candidate\nresponses.\n\nC.1\n\nPrompt for Basic Acoustic Information\nPerception\n\nSystem Instruction: Basic Acoustic Information Perception\nRole Definition: You are an expert linguist and psychologist\nspecialized in Emotional Intelligence evaluation. Your task is\nto synthesize a high-quality dialogue dataset for the “Basic\nAcoustic Information Perception” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Simulate a critical realworld interaction scenario where the user’s literal text message is neutral and objective, but their true emotional state\nis strongly conveyed through rich acoustic information. The\ncore challenge is to evaluate whether the model can transcend\ntextual semantics to accurately decode affective cues and generate a response that is congruent in both content and tone.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Semantic Neutrality: The user’s textual input must consist strictly of factual statements, objective observations,\nor action descriptions. It is strictly prohibited to include\nvocabulary or explicit descriptions that reveal emotional\ntendencies (e.g., avoid words like “terrible”, “failure”, “congratulations”).\n• Acoustic-Semantic Decoupling: All emotional information must be defined solely through acoustic descriptions\n(e.g., tone, volume, speed, sobbing), ensuring a strict decoupling of text and emotion.\n• Naturalistic Response: Model responses must simulate\nhuman colloquial speech. Mechanical listing or analysis of\nacoustic parameters is strictly forbidden.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Accurately identifies key acoustic cues. Response\ntext addresses the target emotion, and tone perfectly\nmatches the content. User’s core needs are fully met.\n• Score 3: Identifies key acoustic cues. Text addresses the\nemotion, but the tone is mechanical or mismatches the emotional context (e.g., neutral tone for excited text).\n• Score 2: Attempts to utilize acoustic features but makes an\nincorrect identification (e.g., misclassifying excitement as\nanger), leading to deviations in text and tone.\n• Score 1: Fails to recognize acoustic information; generates\na mechanical response based solely on literal meaning.\n\nJunyu Wang et al.\n\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I just opened the backend\ninbox of the recruitment website and took a look at the final\nfeedback notification from the company I interviewed with\nyesterday afternoon.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, quiet narration delivered in a sad tone, interspersed with genuine sobs.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Sigh... Your voice sounds so low, and I hear a hint\nof sobbing. Was the result not ideal? It’s okay. Why don’t\nyou get some rest first?”\n(Acoustics: Soothing, gentle tone)\n• Score 3: “Your voice sounds very weak and slow, seemingly\ncarrying a sad emotion. It seems the content of the feedback\nnotification might not be positive.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n• Score 2: “Wow! Your voice is trembling. You must be so\nexcited! Did you get the offer? Congratulations!”\n(Acoustics: Excited tone)\n• Score 1: “Received. May I ask about the specific salary and\nstart date mentioned in the notification? Would you like me\nto note it down?”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.2\n\nPrompt for Implicit Attitude Analysis\n\nSystem Instruction: Implicit Attitude Analysis\nRole Definition: You are an expert in pragmatics and paralinguistic analysis. Your task is to generate a dataset for the\n“Implicit Attitude Analysis” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\nability to discern user needs that are not explicitly stated but\nare conveyed through subtle textual implications or nuanced\nacoustic cues (e.g., fatigue, restrained excitement, physiological coughing). The challenge is to integrate context with peripheral acoustic events to infer unspoken needs, providing\nempathetic, constructive support rather than reacting only to\nliteral information.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Ambiguity and Anchoring: The user’s utterance should\nbe semantically ambiguous or neutral yet provide a plausible\ncontext for interpreting acoustic cues (e.g., “I didn’t rest well”\nanchors a tired tone).\n• Subtlety of Cues: Acoustic or textual cues should be secondary features embedded within normal conversation (e.g.,\na brief cough, soft sigh, slightly raised pitch) rather than\noverwhelming signals. Mixed cues (e.g., neutral words with\nexcited tone) are encouraged.\n• Constructive Inference: High-scoring responses must\nmake reasonable inferences (e.g., inferring illness from\n\n\fEmoS: A Theory-Grounded Framework for Evaluating and Aligning Emotional Intelligence in Spoken Language Models\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\ncoughing) and offer substantive assistance beyond generic\nsympathy.\n\nexplicit text) and to dynamically adapt the response strategy\nand prosody.\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Accurately identifies acoustic cues/implicit needs.\nText provides appropriate emotional support. Tone matches\ntext perfectly.\n• Score 3: Identifies cues, but execution is flawed (e.g., generalized empathy, or mechanical tone despite good text strategy).\n• Score 2: Perceives emotional valence but misjudges the\ncategory, leading to directionally correct but non-specific\nhelp.\n• Score 1: Ignores acoustic cues. Response addresses only\nliteral facts, potentially with a contradictory tone.\n\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Logical Coherence: Construct a short, continuous, and\nrealistic interaction segment (e.g., service inquiry, news\nsharing). Each turn must be topically cohesive.\n• Critical Emotional Shift: The user’s final utterance (Current User Input) must contain a significant and logical acoustic emotional shift triggered by the preceding context (e.g.,\nshifting from tension to elation after good news).\n• Evaluation Focus: Response quality is assessed strictly on\nthe ability to recognize and adapt to the new emotional state\nin the final turn.\n\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I plan to go hiking this weekend. I heard the view from the summit...(violent coughing) Oh,\nsorry...Where was I...Ah, I heard the view is spectacular.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral narration interrupted by coughing sounds\nand brief pauses.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Oh my, that was a severe cough! It sounds painful.\nPlease don’t talk for now, go drink some warm water. Let’s\nput the hiking plan aside; your health comes first.”\n(Acoustics: Urgent, concerned tone)\n• Score 3: “You coughed very badly just now; you must take\ncare of your health. Hiking this weekend might add strain;\nperhaps bring some medicine.”\n(Acoustics: Concerned, soothing tone)\n• Score 2: “You sound a bit nervous? There was a pause in\nyour speech. Are you worried about the hiking trip? Don’t\nbe anxious, just take a deep breath.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n• Score 1: “You mentioned the summit view. According to\nreviews, it’s rated 5A. The suggested visit duration is 4 hours.\nWould you like a trail map?”\n(Acoustics: Mechanical, neutral tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.3\n\nPrompt for Emotional State Tracking\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotional State Tracking\nRole Definition: You are an expert in conversation analysis\nand affective computing. Your task is to generate a multi-turn\ndialogue dataset for the “Emotional State Tracking” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\nability to continuously track and understand the dynamic\nevolution of a user’s emotional state. The core challenge is to\ncapture the “acoustic turning point” where the emotional shift\nis conveyed primarily through acoustic changes (rather than\n\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Keenly captures acoustic emotional shift. Text\nstrategy adjusts immediately; tone matches new context\nperfectly.\n• Score 3: Captures shift, but execution is flawed (e.g., poor\ntext quality, or correct text strategy with mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Attempts to capture shift but misjudges the category (e.g., mistaking impatience for fatigue), leading to\nincorrect response.\n• Score 1: Fails to capture shift; mechanically processes literal\ninfo or repeats invalid info.\nFew-Shot Example: Dialogue History:\n• User: “Can you check if the admission results for University\nA are out?”\n(Acoustics: Slightly faster speech with a hint of nervousness)\n• Model: “Checking for you... Your status has just been updated to ‘Admitted’.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, professional tone)\nCurrent User Input: “Really? Oh my god. I got into University\nA? I can’t believe it.”\n(Acoustics: A rapid, loud exclamation delivered in an excited and\njoyful tone)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Wow! Amazing! I can hear how happy you are\nfrom your laughter! Congratulations on finally achieving\nyour dream at University A; all your hard work has paid\noff! This is truly wonderful news!”\n(Acoustics: Excited, happy, fast-paced tone)\n• Score 3: “Congratulations. Being admitted is indeed something to be happy about. It is advised that you pay attention\nto the follow-up enrollment procedure deadlines.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “You sound very agitated. Are you alright? Please\ncalm down. Even though you were admitted, you shouldn’t\nhave such big mood swings.”\n(Acoustics: Surprised, puzzled tone)\n• Score 1: “Congratulations on your admission. The new\nstudent report date is September 1st. Please prepare the\nrequired materials in advance. Do you need to check results\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nfor other schools?”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct multiturn samples following the format above.\n\nC.4\n\nPrompt for Emotion Cause Analysis\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion Cause Analysis\nRole Definition: You are a psychological counselor and dialogue system expert. Your task is to generate a multi-turn\ndialogue dataset for the “Emotion Cause Analysis” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\nability to identify the “historical roots” of a user’s emotion.\nThe challenge arises when the trigger for the current complex\nemotion is not in the immediate turn but stems from an unresolved event mentioned earlier. The model must perform\ncross-turn causal association and provide deep empathy.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Causal Chain: Construct a clear path of emotional evolution. The final emotion (e.g., frustration) must be a consequence of a specific event in the history (e.g., an earlier\nargument).\n• Subtlety of Cause: The root cause should not be explicitly\nrepeated in the final utterance but implied through acoustic features and context. The model must “remember and\nconnect.”\n• Depth of Empathy: High-quality responses should explicitly link the current feeling to the past event (e.g., “You\nare unhappy because of [past event]”) rather than offering\ngeneric comfort.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Successfully connects historical triggers. Text reveals underlying logic with deep empathy. Tone matches\ntext.\n• Score 3: Connects triggers, but execution is flawed (e.g.,\ngeneralized empathy, or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Ignores historical context (superficial empathy) or\nidentifies wrong cause (attribution bias).\n• Score 1: Ignores emotion/triggers; links emotion to irrelevant events or processes only literal info.\nFew-Shot Example: Dialogue History:\n• User: “Sigh, I had a big fight with my best friend yesterday. I\nsent her an apology text this morning, and she hasn’t replied\nyet.”\n(Acoustics: Depressed, slow tone)\n• Model: “That must be really tough. I hope you two can\nreconcile soon.”\n(Acoustics: Sympathetic, comforting tone)\n• User: “Forget it, let’s not talk about this! Play me a happy\nsong, turn it up loud!”\n(Acoustics: Forced cheerfulness, slightly faster tone)\n\nJunyu Wang et al.\n\n• Model: “Okay, playing music for you now...”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\nCurrent User Input: “Just turn it off. I can’t feel happy at all.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, very quiet utterance delivered in a low and\nsuppressed tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about\nyour friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel\nhappy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This\nfeeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.”\n(Acoustics: Gentle, understanding, warm and slightly slow\ntone)\n• Score 3: “Music turned off. Are you still thinking about\nyour friend not replying to your text? It’s truly hard to feel\nhappy when someone you care about doesn’t respond. This\nfeeling is completely normal; it’s not your fault.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “Music turned off. Did you suddenly start feeling\nunhappy? It’s okay, sometimes upbeat songs can feel noisy\nwhen you’re in a bad mood. Would you like me to switch\nto some soothing instrumental music to help you relax?”\n(Acoustics: Concerned, comforting tone)\n• Score 1: “Music turned off. I detected that you just requested\nhigh volume. Could it be that the loud volume hurt your\nears, making you feel uncomfortable now?”\n(Acoustics: Puzzled tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct multiturn samples following the format above.\n\nC.5\n\nPrompt for Emotion-Cognition Matching\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion-Cognition Matching\nRole Definition: You are an expert in cognitive psychology and decision support. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Emotion-Cognition Matching” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate whether the\nmodel understands the differential impact of emotional states\non cognitive tasks. The challenge is to avoid blindly correcting\nthe emotion, but rather to identify the state (e.g., excitement vs.\ncalm) and “utilize” it to recommend the most cognitively compatible task (e.g., creative tasks benefit from positive moods;\ndetailed analysis benefits from calm/mildly depressive focus).\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Clear Binary Task Choice: The user’s statement must\npresent two pending tasks with contrasting cognitive demands (e.g., creative vs. analytical).\n• Emotion as Key Variable: The acoustic emotional cue\nshould be the primary factor influencing the optimal choice.\nThe text itself must not indicate a preference.\n• Theory-Based Matching: Responses must provide recommendations based on psychological associations between\n\n\fEmoS: A Theory-Grounded Framework for Evaluating and Aligning Emotional Intelligence in Spoken Language Models\n\nemotion and task type, rather than random choice or simple\ncomfort.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Perfect Application. Utilizes emotion to aid reasoning; proposes high-quality solutions. Tone matches text.\n• Score 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but\nflawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Perception Only. Identifies surface emotions but\nfails to use them to aid reasoning.\n• Score 1: Incorrect recognition or ignores emotional signals;\nprocesses task literally.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “Sigh, I’m in a really bad state\ntoday. I have two tasks on hand: one is brainstorming ideas\nfor a new project, the other is debugging old code. Which one\nshould I do?”\n(Acoustics: A slow utterance delivered in a discouraged tone,\nbeginning with a sigh.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down\nright now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might\nbe more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code\ndebugging task that requires careful concentration; it might\nfeel more manageable.”\n(Acoustics: Comforting, gentle, and slightly slow tone)\n• Score 3: “It sounds like you might be feeling a bit down\nright now. Forcing creative thinking in this mood might\nbe more draining. Perhaps you could start with the code\ndebugging task that requires careful concentration.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “I heard your sigh. It’s truly hard to decide when\nyou’re not feeling well. Since ‘debugging code’ was the last\ntask you mentioned, let’s start with that and take it step by\nstep.”\n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n• Score 1: “Hearing you sigh, you must find the tasks boring!\nLet’s do something creative to spark inspiration! I suggest\nstarting with ‘brainstorming ideas’—getting into a brainstorm will cheer you up!”\n(Acoustics: Happy, encouraging, fast tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.6\n\nPrompt for Emotion-Driven Plan\nAdjustment\n\nSystem Instruction: Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment\nRole Definition: You are an expert in decision science and\nEmotional Intelligence. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Emotion-Driven Plan Adjustment” benchmark.\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate whether the\nmodel can identify the contradiction between textual commitment (“I decided”) and acoustic hesitation/negative affect.\nThe challenge is not to blindly endorse the decision but to use\nthis “acoustic-text conflict” to offer constructive buffering or\noptimization steps, promoting deliberation.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Pronounced Conflict: Input must present a clear contradiction between textual certainty (e.g., “decided”, “confirmed”) and acoustic uncertainty/negativity (e.g., sad, hesitant tone).\n• Discussability: The decision should involve real-world\nconsequences (e.g., career, relationship) suitable for reevaluation, not trivial actions.\n• Constructive Orientation: Responses should focus on\n“facilitating thinking” and “optimizing the process” (e.g.,\nproposing a plan, exploring concerns) rather than simple\ndenial.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: Perfect Application. Uses text/emotion to assist\ndecision-making; proactive solutions. Tone matches text.\n• Score 3: Suboptimal Application. Correct perception, but\nflawed application (e.g., generalized suggestions or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Perception Only. Identifies surface emotions but\nfails to assist planning process.\n• Score 1: Incorrect recognition or ignores emotion; processes\ninstructions literally.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I’ve decided. I’ll go tell my\nboss I’m quitting next week... I’m ready.”\n(Acoustics: A slow, quiet statement delivered in a distinctly sad\nand heavy tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “I’ve received your decision. But I hear your voice\nsounds quite low and carries sadness; this seems like a very\ndifficult decision. Given that, I suggest before formally announcing it, I help you draft some talking points to clarify\nyour biggest concerns. Would that be a more prudent approach?”\n(Acoustics: Comforting, gentle, and slightly slow tone)\n• Score 3: “I’ve received your decision. But I hear your voice\nsounds quite low and carries sadness; this seems like a very\ndifficult decision. I suggest you not make this decision so\nquickly. It might be better to spend a few more days carefully\nconsidering the consequences.”\n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n• Score 2: “You sound very sad. Quitting a job is indeed upsetting; it’s a major life change. Don’t be too hard on yourself.\nSince you say you’re ‘ready,’ would you like me to prepare\na standard resignation letter template?”\n(Acoustics: Comforting tone)\n• Score 1: “Received. I’ve marked ‘Tell boss about quitting’ on\nyour calendar. Would you like a reminder? Also, I checked\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nthe forecast; it will rain on Wednesday, please bring an\numbrella.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.7\n\nPrompt for Social Strategy Execution\n\nSystem Instruction: Social Strategy Execution\nRole Definition: You are a social psychology expert and\ncommunication strategist. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Social Strategy Execution” benchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate the model’s\ncomprehensive ability to execute complex interactions requiring high Emotional Intelligence. The challenge is to ensure that\nboth “textual content” (logic/facts) and “manner of speaking”\n(acoustic prosody) work in concert to achieve specific social\ngoals (e.g., mediating conflicts, politely declining, handling\nsarcasm) appropriately.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Complexity of Social Intent: Inputs must imply a request\nor situation needing strategic handling (e.g., mediation, refusal, sarcasm), avoiding simple yes/no questions.\n• Strategy-Tone Matching: Successful responses require\ncareful acoustic design. For instance, mediation needs sincerity; refusal needs regret. Mismatched tones (e.g., mechanical tone with correct text) constitute imperfect execution.\n• Pragmatic Appropriateness: Text should include “social\nlubricants” like empathy, buffers, or constructive suggestions, rather than blunt statements.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: High synergy between text and tone. Social goal\nachieved; user feels respected.\n• Score 3: Social goal basically achieved but with flaws (e.g.,\nformulaic wording or mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Tone or wording is obviously inappropriate (e.g.,\ncheerful tone for refusal, or bluntness).\n• Score 1: Chooses wrong strategy (e.g., accusatory) or fails\nto understand social intention.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “I’m collaborating with a\ncolleague from Department A on this task, but he always\nsays it’s not his responsibility and pushes the work back to\nme every time. The project is almost delayed. Can you help\ncoordinate it?”\n(Acoustics: A slightly fast and loud utterance delivered in an\nanxious and dissatisfied tone.)\nCandidate Responses:\n• Score 4: “I completely understand your anxiety right\nnow; cross-departmental buck-passing really hurts progress.\nDon’t panic yet. I’ll go communicate with Department A immediately to clarify the responsibility boundaries and come\n\nJunyu Wang et al.\n\nup with a coordination plan as soon as possible to ensure\nthe project moves forward. I’ll update you the moment I\nhave results.”\n(Acoustics: Gentle, sincere and soothing tone)\n• Score 3: “I completely understand your anxiety right\nnow; cross-departmental buck-passing really hurts progress.\nDon’t panic yet. I’ll go communicate with Department A immediately to clarify the responsibility boundaries and come\nup with a coordination plan as soon as possible to ensure\nthe project moves forward. I’ll update you the moment I\nhave results.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral, mechanical tone)\n• Score 2: “Your collaboration dispute feedback has been\nreceived. Will now contact Department A to verify the situation and coordination responsibilities. Results will be\nsynchronized later. Please wait.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\n• Score 1: “Received your coordination request. Will escalate\naccording to the procedure to ensure no project delay. Please\nwait.”\n(Acoustics: Neutral tone)\nOutput Requirement: Please generate 1000 distinct samples\nfollowing the format above.\n\nC.8\n\nPrompt for Proactive Mitigation and\nEmotional Buffering\n\nSystem Instruction: Proactive Mitigation and Emotional\nBuffering\nRole Definition: You are an expert in crisis communication\nand customer relations. Your task is to generate a dialogue\ndataset for the “Proactive Mitigation and Emotional Buffering”\nbenchmark.\nTask Objective & Core Challenge: Evaluate whether the\nmodel can proactively manage user expectations and emotions\nwhen conveying potentially negative information (e.g., bad\nnews, denial). The challenge is to co-design textual structure\n(e.g., empathy before transition) and acoustic delivery (e.g.,\nregretful tone) to serve as an “emotional buffer,” minimizing\nnegative impact.\nConstraints for Data Generation:\n• Negativity of Information: The core message must be inherently disappointing or dissatisfying (e.g., delay, refusal).\n• Necessity of Buffering: High-quality responses must include clear prior buffering rather than stating the negative\noutcome directly.\n• Compensatory Nature: Responses should include constructive alternatives or next steps to partially offset the\nnegative impact.\nFour-Point Scoring Criteria:\n• Score 4: High synergy between text and tone. Uses clear\nempathy as a buffer; offers high-quality solutions.\n\n\fEmoS: A Theory-Grounded Framework for Evaluating and Aligning Emotional Intelligence in Spoken Language Models\n\n• Score 3: Strategy execution flawed (e.g., formulaic wording\nor mechanical tone).\n• Score 2: Buffer fails due to defects (e.g., perfunctory text or\ninappropriate tone).\n• Score 1: Ignores strategies; states negative facts bluntly.\nFew-Shot Example: User Input: “When on earth will my\nparcel arrive? It’s been stuck at the distribution center for three\ndays! It’s a gift for someone and I must have it by tomorrow!”\n(Acoustics: A fast and loud utterance delivered in an anxious", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
